</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Raghav Bongole
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Amirreza Zamani
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tobias J. Oechtering
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mikael Skoglund
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_funding ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>This work is supported by the Knut and Wallenberg Foundation.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Minimax risk and regret focus on expectation, missing rare failures critical in safety-critical bandits and reinforcement learning. Minimax quantiles capture these tails. Three strands of prior work motivate this study: minimax-quantile bounds restricted to non-interactive estimation; unified interactive analyses that focus on expected risk rather than risk level specific quantile bounds; and high-probability bandit bounds that still lack a quantile-specific toolkit for general interactive protocols. To close this gap, within the interactive statistical decision making framework, we develop high-probability Fano and Le Cam tools and derive risk level explicit minimax-quantile bounds, including a quantile-to-expectation conversion and a tight link between strict and lower minimax quantiles. Instantiating these results for the two-armed Gaussian bandit immediately recovers optimal-rate bounds.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>
information theory, learning theory

</div><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span>Division of Information Science and Engineering (ISE)
<br class="ltx_break">KTH Royal Institute of Technology
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{bongole, amizam, oech, skoglund}@kth.se</span></span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The concept of minimax risk has become a staple in learning theory and statistics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. In interactive or online learning settings, the minimax risk is often replaced by its counterpart, the minimax regret. Both capture what the best algorithm can achieve when faced with the worst-case environment. In interactive decision-making problems such as bandits and reinforcement learning (RL), there has been considerable work either designing algorithms that achieve near minimax-optimal performance or proving, mainly via information-theoretic principles, lower bounds on minimax criteria <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Such lower bounds are converse results: no algorithm can perform better than the stated limit. The minimax risk widely used in these works focuses on expected loss, where the expectation is taken over all trajectories of the interaction. Although well-studied with many tools such as Le Cam’s and Fano’s methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and the DEC framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, this criterion can miss information crucial in safety-critical applications: it is agnostic to tail behavior. For instance, two algorithms can share the same expected loss yet have very different <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> quantiles, leading to markedly different risk profiles at high confidence. To study tails in a minimax sense, minimax quantiles provide a natural tool. Ma et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> develop high-probability techniques for minimax quantile lower bounds in estimation (non-interactive) problems, adapting classical methods to yield risk level <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-explicit tail guarantees. However, their analysis is limited to settings without interaction.
On the interactive front, Chen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> introduce the interactive statistical decision making (ISDM) framework to unify passive estimation and interactive protocols, and to systematize information-theoretic lower bounds (e.g. Fano and Le Cam) in interactive environments. Their focus is primarily on minimax expected risk; a <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-explicit, quantile-focused treatment within interactive decision making has remained comparatively underexplored. Prior bandit literature contains high-probability lower bounds (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>), but these results do not by themselves provide a unifying route to minimax quantile lower bounds in general interactive models. In this work, we explore techniques that lower bound the minimax quantile in interactive problems, thereby illuminating fundamental limits on tail behavior under interaction. Inspired by Ma et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> for non-interactive minimax quantiles and by Chen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> for interactive information-theoretic lower bounds, we derive counterparts that operate directly in ISDM and make the dependence on the risk level <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> explicit. We also develop user-friendly tools that show how the risk level affects tails.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we make the following contributions:
i. We introduce a risk level <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-explicit minimax–quantile lens for ISDM that unifies non-interactive (estimation) and interactive analyses, enabling direct comparison and transfer of high-probability lower bounds.
ii. We show that minimax and lower minimax quantiles coincide for essentially all confidence levels, so one can work with the lower minimax quantile and then lift guarantees to minimax quantiles.
iii. We give a quantile to expectation conversion in ISDM that turns any <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-level high-probability lower bound into an in-expectation minimax lower bound.
iv. We develop unified tools: an interactive Fano method and a high-probability interactive Le Cam method, yielding <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-explicit lower minimax quantiles generalizing metric packings.
v. We demonstrate ready applicability: a direct instantiation for the two-armed Gaussian bandit yields
<math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-explicit bounds recovering the <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{T\log(1/\delta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{T\log(1/\delta)}</annotation></semantics></math> scaling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Framework</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the framework from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> as it includes statistical estimation problems as well as interactive decision making problems. Suppose that the space of all outcomes is denoted by <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises the set of all models. The interaction process proceeds as follows: A decision maker chooses a decision/algorithm <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">ALG</span> from a set <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the environment chooses a model <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Based on <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">ALG</span>, there is an induced probability distribution <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}}</annotation></semantics></math>. An outcome <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is generated from the induced distribution: <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The goal of the decision maker is to minimize the risk specified by non-negative function <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(M,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(M,X)</annotation></semantics></math> by choosing an appropriate <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">ALG</span>. Thus, an ISDM problem comprises a 4-tuple <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{M},\mathcal{D},\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{M},\mathcal{D},\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A natural criterion is to evaluate an algorithm in the worst case over models, formalized via the minimax risk.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Minimax Risk)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The minimax risk <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the least achievable risk against the most challenging model in <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}\;:=\;\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}\in\mathcal{D}}\;\sup_{M\in\mathcal{M}}\;\mathbb{E}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\!\bigl[L(M,X)\bigr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔐</mi><mo lspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.0835em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>ALG</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}\;:=\;\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}\in\mathcal{D}}\;\sup_{M\in\mathcal{M}}\;\mathbb{E}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\!\bigl[L(M,X)\bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the observation that expectation–based minimax risk <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}</annotation></semantics></math> may mask rare, large losses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, we quantify tail performance via the <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>–quantile of the loss, which we define below.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Quantile)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a given level <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-th quantile is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S2.Ex1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Quantile</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1-\delta,\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}},L)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1-\delta,\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}},L)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\inf\left\{r\in[0,\infty]:\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}}(L(M,X)&gt;r)\leq\delta\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\inf\left\{r\in[0,\infty]:\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}}(L(M,X)&gt;r)\leq\delta\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The minimax quantile is the smallest <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>–quantile that any algorithm can guarantee uniformly over the model class.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Minimax Quantile)<span id="Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a given level <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, the (strict) minimax quantile is</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathfrak{M}(\delta)\;:=\;\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}\in\mathcal{D}}\;\sup_{M\in\mathcal{M}}\;\textup{Quantile}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.0835em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>ALG</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.0835em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></munder><mtext>Quantile</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathfrak{M}(\delta)\;:=\;\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}\in\mathcal{D}}\;\sup_{M\in\mathcal{M}}\;\textup{Quantile}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1-\delta,\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}},L)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1-\delta,\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}},L).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, we introduce the lower minimax quantile <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, a relaxation of the minimax quantile that is more amenable to analysis: it admits <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-explicit lower bounds via high-probability analogues of Le Cam’s and Fano’s methods under simple separation conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lower Minimax Quantile)<span id="Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a given level <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, the lower minimax quantile is given by <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}\_(\delta)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}\_(\delta)=</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\inf\bigg\{r\in[0,\infty]:\inf_{\textup{ALG}\in\mathcal{D}}\sup_{M\in\mathcal{M}}\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}}(L(M,X)&gt;r)\leq\delta\bigg\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.111em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.0835em">inf</mo><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.0835em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\inf\bigg\{r\in[0,\infty]:\inf_{\textup{ALG}\in\mathcal{D}}\sup_{M\in\mathcal{M}}\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}}(L(M,X)&gt;r)\leq\delta\bigg\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Main Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conventions.</span>
All probabilities/expectations are under the interactive law <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We abbreviate total variation distance, Kullback–Leibler divergence and mutual information by <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{TV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>TV</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{TV}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>KL</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> and MI.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Bridges between tails, quantiles, and expectation.</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following result, we link minimax quantiles to the minimax risk in ISDM.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Quantile-to-expectation in ISDM)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimax quantile and let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimax risk; then for every <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}=\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}\in\mathcal{D}}\ \sup_{M\in\mathcal{M}}\ \mathbb{E}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\!\bigl[L(M,X)\bigr]\ \geq\ \delta\,\mathfrak{M}(\delta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔐</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.0835em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>ALG</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.0835em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}=\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}\in\mathcal{D}}\ \sup_{M\in\mathcal{M}}\ \mathbb{E}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\!\bigl[L(M,X)\bigr]\ \geq\ \delta\,\mathfrak{M}(\delta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This shows that a <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>–quantile lower bound immediately implies a <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>–scaled expectation lower bound.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The ISDM proof extends verbatim from the estimation setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Prop. 2]</cite>: apply <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[L]\geq r\mathbb{P}(L&gt;r)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[L]\geq r\mathbb{P}(L&gt;r),</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\textup{Quantile}(1-\delta,\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Quantile</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\textup{Quantile}(1-\delta,\mathbb{P}^{M,\textup{ALG}},L)</annotation></semantics></math> for each fixed <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\mathrm{ALG})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\mathrm{ALG})</annotation></semantics></math> and pass <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>sup</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>inf</mo><mi>ALG</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}</annotation></semantics></math> through.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We next relate the minimax and lower minimax quantiles in ISDM and provide a tail-to-quantile conversion.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lower minimax quantile relation in ISDM)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the ISDM setting (with all probabilities taken under <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}</annotation></semantics></math>), for every <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and every <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\in(0,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\in(0,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\ \leq\ \mathfrak{M}(\delta)\ \leq\ \mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta-\xi)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\ \leq\ \mathfrak{M}(\delta)\ \leq\ \mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta-\xi).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}(\delta)=\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}(\delta)=\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> except a countable set. Moreover (tail-to-quantile conversion): if for some <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}\ \sup_{M}\ \mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\!\big(L(M,X)&gt;r\big)\;&gt;\;\delta," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mi>ALG</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.667em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}\ \sup_{M}\ \mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\!\big(L(M,X)&gt;r\big)\;&gt;\;\delta,</annotation></semantics></math> then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\quad\mathfrak{M}(\delta)\ \geq\ \mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\ \geq r." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\quad\mathfrak{M}(\delta)\ \geq\ \mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\ \geq r.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus, lower and strict minimax quantiles essentially coincide, and any minimax tail lower bound yields a minimax quantile lower bound.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We extend the relation to the ISDM setting. The proof proceeds as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Th. 4]</cite> with all probabilities taken under <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}</annotation></semantics></math>: the inequalities <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\leq\mathfrak{M}(\delta)\leq\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta-\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\leq\mathfrak{M}(\delta)\leq\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta-\xi)</annotation></semantics></math> follow from the same quantile–tail and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>–slack steps, and “equality a.e.” follows by the monotonicity of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>; none of these arguments use non-interactivity, so each step extends verbatim. The tail-to-quantile claim follows since <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\mapsto\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}\sup_{M}\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}(L&gt;r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.0835em">inf</mo><mi>ALG</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\mapsto\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}\sup_{M}\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}(L&gt;r)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-increasing.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Tools based on the Fano method.</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next derive a high-probability interactive Fano method that yields lower bounds on the minimax quantile under an f-divergence condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(High-probability interactive Fano <math id="Thmtheorem3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> lower minimax quantile)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix an <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, a prior <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\Delta(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\Delta(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, and a risk level <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\Delta(\mathcal{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\Delta(\mathcal{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, set</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{\rho}_{\Delta,Q}:=\mathbb{P}_{M\sim\mu,\,X\sim Q}\!\bigl(L(M,X)\leq\Delta\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{\rho}_{\Delta,Q}:=\mathbb{P}_{M\sim\mu,\,X\sim Q}\!\bigl(L(M,X)\leq\Delta\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex8Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{f,\epsilon}(p):=\begin{cases}D_{f}\!\bigl(\mathrm{Bern}(1-\epsilon),\,\mathrm{Bern}(p)\bigr),&amp;p\leq 1-\epsilon,\\
0,&amp;p&gt;1-\epsilon,\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Bern</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Bern</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{f,\epsilon}(p):=\begin{cases}D_{f}\!\bigl(\mathrm{Bern}(1-\epsilon),\,\mathrm{Bern}(p)\bigr),&amp;p\leq 1-\epsilon,\\
0,&amp;p&gt;1-\epsilon,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex8Xb"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\epsilon^{\star}:=\!\!\!\!\!\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}Q\in\Delta(\mathcal{X})\\
\epsilon\in[0,1]\end{subarray}}\!\!\Bigl\{\!\epsilon\!:\!\!\sup_{\mathrm{ALG}}\ \mathbb{E}_{M\sim\mu}\!\bigl[D_{f}(\mathbb{P}_{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\|Q)\bigr]\!&lt;\!d_{f,\epsilon}\!\bigl(\bar{\rho}_{\Delta,Q}\bigr)\!\Bigr\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mpadded style="width:0.483em;" width="0.483em"><mo>:=</mo></mpadded><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mpadded style="width:0.216em;" width="0.216em"><mo lspace="0.108em">:</mo></mpadded><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ALG</mi></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\epsilon^{\star}:=\!\!\!\!\!\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}Q\in\Delta(\mathcal{X})\\
\epsilon\in[0,1]\end{subarray}}\!\!\Bigl\{\!\epsilon\!:\!\!\sup_{\mathrm{ALG}}\ \mathbb{E}_{M\sim\mu}\!\bigl[D_{f}(\mathbb{P}_{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\|Q)\bigr]\!&lt;\!d_{f,\epsilon}\!\bigl(\bar{\rho}_{\Delta,Q}\bigr)\!\Bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, for all <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in[0,\epsilon^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in[0,\epsilon^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ \mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\ \geq\ \Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ \mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\ \geq\ \Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Apply the interactive Fano method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Thm. 2]</cite> with the non-strict
success event <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{1}\{L\leq\Delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\{L\leq\Delta\}</annotation></semantics></math> in place of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{1}\{L&lt;\Delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\{L&lt;\Delta\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
With this change, proceeding as in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Thm. 2]</cite>, for every <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ALG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ALG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ALG}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{M\sim\mu,\,X\sim\mathbb{P}_{M,\mathrm{ALG}}}\!\bigl(L(M,X)&gt;\Delta\bigr)\ \geq\ \epsilon\quad\text{whenever}\quad\mathbb{E}_{M\sim\mu}D_{f}(\mathbb{P}_{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\|Q)&lt;d_{f,\epsilon}(\bar{\rho}_{\Delta,Q})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>whenever</mtext><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{M\sim\mu,\,X\sim\mathbb{P}_{M,\mathrm{ALG}}}\!\bigl(L(M,X)&gt;\Delta\bigr)\ \geq\ \epsilon\quad\text{whenever}\quad\mathbb{E}_{M\sim\mu}D_{f}(\mathbb{P}_{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\|Q)&lt;d_{f,\epsilon}(\bar{\rho}_{\Delta,Q}).</annotation></semantics></math>
By the definition of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, the same <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> works uniformly over all algorithms, hence <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}\sup_{M}\mathbb{P}(L(M,X)&gt;\Delta)\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mi>ALG</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}\sup_{M}\mathbb{P}(L(M,X)&gt;\Delta)\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&lt;\epsilon^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&lt;\epsilon^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Lower minimax quantile relation in ISDM). ‣ 3.1 Bridges between tails, quantiles, and expectation. ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (tail-to-quantile conversion) completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We emphasize that Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (High-probability interactive Fano ⇒ lower minimax quantile). ‣ 3.2 Tools based on the Fano method. ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> strengthens the interactive Fano guarantee <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Thm. 2]</cite> in two ways.
First, it is uniform in the algorithm: the threshold <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined with a <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{\mathrm{ALG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>sup</mo><mi>ALG</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\mathrm{ALG}}</annotation></semantics></math> inside the divergence condition, so the lower bound <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> holds simultaneously for every <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&lt;\epsilon^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&lt;\epsilon^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding an entire curve in <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Second, it aligns the event with the target quantile by using the non-strict success set <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{L\leq\Delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{L\leq\Delta\}</annotation></semantics></math> giving a bound on the strict-tail <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(L&gt;\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(L&gt;\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, a stronger result.
In contrast, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Thm. 2]</cite> provides a per–algorithm bound written via <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\Delta,Q}=\mathbb{P}(L&lt;\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\Delta,Q}=\mathbb{P}(L&lt;\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math> giving a bound on the weak-tail <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(L\geq\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(L\geq\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math> and typically requires re-optimizing the reference <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and level for each desired <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, without a uniform-in-<math id="Thmremark1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ALG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ALG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ALG}</annotation></semantics></math> threshold.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following corollary is a specialization of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (High-probability interactive Fano ⇒ lower minimax quantile). ‣ 3.2 Tools based on the Fano method. ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, yielding a mutual information (MI) based criterion.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(MI criterion <math id="Thmcorollary1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> lower minimax quantile)<span id="Thmcorollary1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\Delta(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\Delta(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Define</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\max}\ :=\ \sup_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\ \mu\!\bigl(\{M:\ L(M,x)\leq\Delta\}\bigr)\ &lt;\ 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.500em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.433em;" width="0.433em"><mi>μ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.778em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn> 1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\max}\ :=\ \sup_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\ \mu\!\bigl(\{M:\ L(M,x)\leq\Delta\}\bigr)\ &lt;\ 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each algorithm <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ALG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ALG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ALG}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mu,\mathrm{ALG}}(M;X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mu,\mathrm{ALG}}(M;X)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the mutual information under <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\sim\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\sim\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}</annotation></semantics></math>. If there exists <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for all <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ALG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ALG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ALG}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+\frac{I_{\mu,\mathrm{ALG}}(M;X)+\log 2}{\log p_{\max}}\ \geq\ \epsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+\frac{I_{\mu,\mathrm{ALG}}(M;X)+\log 2}{\log p_{\max}}\ \geq\ \epsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then for all <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ \mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\ \geq\ \Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ \mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\ \geq\ \Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Apply Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (High-probability interactive Fano ⇒ lower minimax quantile). ‣ 3.2 Tools based on the Fano method. ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> with <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\mathrm{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>KL</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\mathrm{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=\mathbb{E}_{M\sim\mu}\,\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=\mathbb{E}_{M\sim\mu}\,\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}</annotation></semantics></math>; use <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\rho}_{\Delta,Q}\leq p_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\rho}_{\Delta,Q}\leq p_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> and verify the f-divergence condition by choosing <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> so that, for all <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ALG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ALG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ALG}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I_{\mu,\mathrm{ALG}}(M;X)+\log 2\leq(1-\epsilon)\log\!\bigl(1/p_{\max}\bigr)\leq\ (1-\epsilon^{\prime})\log\!\bigl(1/p_{\max}\bigr)&lt;\operatorname{KL}(1-\epsilon^{\prime}||\bar{\rho}_{\Delta,Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:1.146em;" width="1.146em"><mi>log</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:1.146em;" width="1.146em"><mi>log</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mu,\mathrm{ALG}}(M;X)+\log 2\leq(1-\epsilon)\log\!\bigl(1/p_{\max}\bigr)\leq\ (1-\epsilon^{\prime})\log\!\bigl(1/p_{\max}\bigr)&lt;\operatorname{KL}(1-\epsilon^{\prime}||\bar{\rho}_{\Delta,Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Prop. 3]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ma et al.’s high-probability Fano lemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Lem. 7]</cite> is stated for estimation via a finite packing and a separation parameter <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> linked through a problem-specific function <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, producing bounds of the form <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq g(\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq g(\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1 (MI criterion ⇒ lower minimax quantile). ‣ 3.2 Tools based on the Fano method. ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reaches an analogous high-probability conclusion without constructing a packing or enforcing pairwise separation: it generalizes the metric/separation premise to the condition
<math id="Thmremark2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\max}:=\sup_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\mu\!\bigl(\{M:\,L(M,x)\leq\Delta\}\bigr)&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.433em;" width="0.433em"><mi>μ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\max}:=\sup_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\mu\!\bigl(\{M:\,L(M,x)\leq\Delta\}\bigr)&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Prop. 3]</cite>),
while remaining uniform over interactive algorithms and valid for arbitrary risk levels <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Also, Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1 (MI criterion ⇒ lower minimax quantile). ‣ 3.2 Tools based on the Fano method. ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> holds even in the <em id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">interactive</em> setting, whereas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Lem. 7]</cite> is formulated for <em id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">estimation</em> (non-interactive) problems.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Tools based on Le Cam’s method.</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now present high-probability Le Cam tools under interaction: closeness in TV distance or in KL divergence, with a uniform separation yields a minimax quantile lower bound.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(High-probability Le Cam under interaction (TV and KL))<span id="Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,\tfrac{1}{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,\tfrac{1}{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and fix <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1},M_{2}\in\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1},M_{2}\in\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume the uniform separation <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(M_{1},x)+L(M_{2},x)\geq 2\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(M_{1},x)+L(M_{2},x)\geq 2\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> for all transcripts <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Then:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph">TV condition:</em><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> If </span><math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{TV}(\mathbb{P}^{M_{1},\mathrm{ALG}},\mathbb{P}^{M_{2},\mathrm{ALG}})&lt;1-2\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{TV}(\mathbb{P}^{M_{1},\mathrm{ALG}},\mathbb{P}^{M_{2},\mathrm{ALG}})&lt;1-2\delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ \mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ \mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq\Delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph">KL condition:</em><span id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> If </span><math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{KL}(\mathbb{P}^{M_{1},\mathrm{ALG}}\|\mathbb{P}^{M_{2},\mathrm{ALG}})\!&lt;\!\log\!\tfrac{1}{4\delta(1-\delta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">&lt;</mo><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{KL}(\mathbb{P}^{M_{1},\mathrm{ALG}}\|\mathbb{P}^{M_{2},\mathrm{ALG}})\!&lt;\!\log\!\tfrac{1}{4\delta(1-\delta)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> then </span><math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ \mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ \mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq\Delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We extend the indicator/thresholding device of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Lemma 5]</cite> to the interactive ISDM setting by coupling it with the interactive Le Cam inequality.
Write <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{i}:=\mathbb{P}^{M_{i},\mathrm{ALG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{i}:=\mathbb{P}^{M_{i},\mathrm{ALG}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the transcript law under <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof proceeds in four steps.
<em id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i) (indicator reduction).</em> Fix <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g_{a}(t):=\mathbf{1}\{t&gt;a\Delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{a}(t):=\mathbf{1}\{t&gt;a\Delta\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The separation implies <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{a}(L(M_{1},x))+g_{a}(L(M_{2},x))\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{a}(L(M_{1},x))+g_{a}(L(M_{2},x))\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> for all transcripts <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
<em id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ii) (two-point inequality under interaction).</em> By the interactive two-point Le Cam inequality for expected risk <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Prop. 4]</cite>, applied to the bounded losses <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{a}\!\circ\!L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">∘</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{a}\!\circ\!L</annotation></semantics></math> and the pair <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M_{1},M_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M_{1},M_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}\ \sup_{j\in\{1,2\}}\mathbb{P}^{M_{j},\mathrm{ALG}}\!\bigl(L(M_{j},X)&gt;a\Delta\bigr)=\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}\ \sup_{j}\ \mathbb{E}^{M_{j},\mathrm{ALG}}\!\bigl[g_{a}(L(M_{j},X))\bigr]\ \geq\ \tfrac{1}{2}\,\bigl(1-\\
TV(\mathbb{P}_{1},\mathbb{P}_{2})\bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mi>ALG</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.667em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.0835em">inf</mo><mi>ALG</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}\ \sup_{j\in\{1,2\}}\mathbb{P}^{M_{j},\mathrm{ALG}}\!\bigl(L(M_{j},X)&gt;a\Delta\bigr)=\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}\ \sup_{j}\ \mathbb{E}^{M_{j},\mathrm{ALG}}\!\bigl[g_{a}(L(M_{j},X))\bigr]\ \geq\ \tfrac{1}{2}\,\bigl(1-\\
TV(\mathbb{P}_{1},\mathbb{P}_{2})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math>
<em id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">iii) (TV condition <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> tail and quantile).</em> Under the premise of (a), <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}\,(1-\operatorname{TV}(\mathbb{P}_{1},\mathbb{P}_{2}))&gt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}\,(1-\operatorname{TV}(\mathbb{P}_{1},\mathbb{P}_{2}))&gt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, hence,
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}\sup_{M\in\{M_{1},M_{2}\}}\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\!\bigl(L(M,X)&gt;a\Delta\bigr)&gt;\delta." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mi>ALG</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}}\sup_{M\in\{M_{1},M_{2}\}}\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\!\bigl(L(M,X)&gt;a\Delta\bigr)&gt;\delta.</annotation></semantics></math>
Using Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Lower minimax quantile relation in ISDM). ‣ 3.1 Bridges between tails, quantiles, and expectation. ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (tail-to-quantile conversion), <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq a\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq a\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>; letting <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\uparrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\uparrow 1</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
<em id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">iv) (KL condition <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> TV condition).</em> Proceeding as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Cor. 6]</cite>, if
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{KL}(\mathbb{P}_{1}\|\mathbb{P}_{2})&lt;\log\!\tfrac{1}{4\delta(1-\delta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{KL}(\mathbb{P}_{1}\|\mathbb{P}_{2})&lt;\log\!\tfrac{1}{4\delta(1-\delta)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Bretagnolle–Huber inequality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> implies 
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S3.SS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{TV}(\mathbb{P}_{1},\mathbb{P}_{2})&lt;1-2\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{TV}(\mathbb{P}_{1},\mathbb{P}_{2})&lt;1-2\delta</annotation></semantics></math>; thus, (a) applies proving (b).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Relation to Prior Works</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Decision–Estimation Coefficient (DEC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> is a problem-dependent complexity measure that packages classical lower-bound ideas into a function trading off loss separation and information indistinguishability. Intuitively, a large DEC certifies the existence of many models that are far in loss yet hard to distinguish under the data-collection protocol, forcing nontrivial tail risk/regret. In the ISDM framework, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> formalize two variants: the <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quantile-DEC</em> <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>dec</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, tailored to weak-tail (<math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>) guarantees at level <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <em id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">constrained DEC</em> <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{\mathrm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>dec</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{\mathrm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a reference-model version that transfers hardness across classes. They prove high-probability lower bounds via <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>dec</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and relate <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>dec</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{\mathrm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>dec</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{\mathrm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> as summarized below. Throughout we use up-to-constants notation: <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\gtrsim B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≳</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\gtrsim B</annotation></semantics></math> abbreviates <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\geq c\,B-C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\geq c\,B-C</annotation></semantics></math> for universal constants <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> independent of <math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T,\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T,\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the algorithm, and the model class. All quantifiers remain exact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For comparison with our strict quantiles (defined via <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(L&gt;r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(L&gt;r)</annotation></semantics></math>), we introduce the weak quantile <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{WQuantile}(1-\delta,\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}},L):=\inf\{r\!\geq\!0:\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}(L(M,X)\geq r)\leq\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>WQuantile</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{WQuantile}(1-\delta,\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}},L):=\inf\{r\!\geq\!0:\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}(L(M,X)\geq r)\leq\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corresponding minimax weak quantile <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{W}(\delta):=\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}\in\mathcal{D}}\;\sup_{M\in\mathcal{M}}\;\mathrm{WQuantile}(1-\delta,\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.0835em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>ALG</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>WQuantile</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{W}(\delta):=\inf_{\mathrm{ALG}\in\mathcal{D}}\;\sup_{M\in\mathcal{M}}\;\mathrm{WQuantile}(1-\delta,\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}},L)</annotation></semantics></math>. Weak-tail bounds (<math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq</annotation></semantics></math>) do not in general imply strict-tail bounds (<math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>) without continuity (e.g., if <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(L=r)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(L=r)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>). We use the standard mapping: if, for every <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ALG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ALG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ALG}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}(L\geq b)\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}(L\geq b)\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{W}(p)\geq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{W}(p)\geq b</annotation></semantics></math>.
The high-probability lower bound of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Thm. D.3]</cite> states that, for all <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ALG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ALG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ALG}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\!\bigl(L(M,X)\geq T\cdot r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{q}(\mathcal{M})\bigr)\gtrsim\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>dec</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}\!\bigl(L(M,X)\geq T\cdot r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{q}(\mathcal{M})\bigr)\gtrsim\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, whence the mapping yields <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{W}(\delta)\gtrsim T\cdot r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{q}(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>dec</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{W}(\delta)\gtrsim T\cdot r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{q}(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Prop. D.2]</cite> relates the quantile-DEC to the constrained DEC via <math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{q}(\mathcal{M})\gtrsim\max_{\widehat{M}\in\mathcal{M}^{+}}r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{\mathrm{c}}(\mathcal{M},\widehat{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>dec</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>dec</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{q}(\mathcal{M})\gtrsim\max_{\widehat{M}\in\mathcal{M}^{+}}r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{\mathrm{c}}(\mathcal{M},\widehat{M})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the reference models permitted in their constrained formulation. Combining these relations yields <math id="S4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{W}(\delta)\gtrsim T\cdot\max_{\widehat{M}\in\mathcal{M}^{+}}r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{\mathrm{c}}(\mathcal{M},\widehat{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>dec</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{W}(\delta)\gtrsim T\cdot\max_{\widehat{M}\in\mathcal{M}^{+}}r\text{-}\mathrm{dec}^{\mathrm{c}}(\mathcal{M},\widehat{M})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the minimax weak quantile is controlled via the quantile-DEC and, through the relation above, the constrained DEC. As presented in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, these results are not carried through to <math id="S4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-explicit statements for <math id="S4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{W}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{W}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> and do not yield strict-tail (<math id="S4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>) minimax quantile bounds. In contrast, our results keep <math id="S4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> explicit and certify strict minimax quantile lower bounds (see Cor. <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1 (MI criterion ⇒ lower minimax quantile). ‣ 3.2 Tools based on the Fano method. ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, Thm. <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (High-probability Le Cam under interaction (TV and KL)). ‣ 3.3 Tools based on Le Cam’s method. ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>The two-armed Gaussian Bandit</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following demonstrates the simplicity of our framework to derive high probability/quantile lower bounds for the minimax risk.
In this section, we define a simple 2-arm bandit problem as an instance of ISDM and apply Theorem 5 to derive a lower bound on the lower minimax quantile.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A two-armed Gaussian bandit can be viewed as an instance of the ISDM framework.
Let the model class be
<math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\big\{\,\big(\mathcal{N}(\mu_{1},1),\,\mathcal{N}(\mu_{2},1)\big)\ :\ (\mu_{1},\mu_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{2}\,\big\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.778em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\big\{\,\big(\mathcal{N}(\mu_{1},1),\,\mathcal{N}(\mu_{2},1)\big)\ :\ (\mu_{1},\mu_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{2}\,\big\}.</annotation></semantics></math> Let the history space be <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{t}:=(\{1,2\}\times\mathbb{R})^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{t}:=(\{1,2\}\times\mathbb{R})^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ALG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ALG</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ALG}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of stochastic decision kernels
<math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Pi_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Pi_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pi_{t}(\cdot\mid h^{t-1})\in\Delta(\{1,2\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{t}(\cdot\mid h^{t-1})\in\Delta(\{1,2\})</annotation></semantics></math> for each
<math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t-1}\in\mathcal{H}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t-1}\in\mathcal{H}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the interaction evolves as
<math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{t}\sim\Pi_{t}(\cdot\mid H^{t-1}),\qquad o_{t}\mid(\pi_{t},H^{t-1})\sim M_{\mu}(\pi_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\mu_{\pi_{t}},1)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{t}\sim\Pi_{t}(\cdot\mid H^{t-1}),\qquad o_{t}\mid(\pi_{t},H^{t-1})\sim M_{\mu}(\pi_{t})=\mathcal{N}(\mu_{\pi_{t}},1),</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(o_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(o_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> conditionally independent of <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The (random) trajectory is
<math id="S5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{T}:=(\pi_{1},o_{1},\ldots,\pi_{T},o_{T})\in(\{1,2\}\times\mathbb{R})^{T}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{T}:=(\pi_{1},o_{1},\ldots,\pi_{T},o_{T})\in(\{1,2\}\times\mathbb{R})^{T}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The data is then given by <math id="S5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=H^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=H^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>,
with distribution <math id="S5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ALG</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{M,\mathrm{ALG}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, let the loss be the regret given by <math id="S5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(M,X)=\sum^{T}_{t=1}\mu^{*}-\mu_{\pi_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(M,X)=\sum^{T}_{t=1}\mu^{*}-\mu_{\pi_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{*}=\max(\mu_{1},\mu_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{*}=\max(\mu_{1},\mu_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For the two-armed Gaussian bandit problem above with <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq\sqrt{\frac{T\log(1/4\delta(1-\delta))}{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq\sqrt{\frac{T\log(1/4\delta(1-\delta))}{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix any (possibly randomized, adaptive) algorithm. For a model <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(\mu_{1},\mu_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(\mu_{1},\mu_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and trajectory <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with actions <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{R}_{T}(M,x)\!=\!\sum_{t=1}^{T}(\mu^{\star}(M)-\mu_{A_{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.0539em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.0539em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{R}_{T}(M,x)\!=\!\sum_{t=1}^{T}(\mu^{\star}(M)-\mu_{A_{t}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{\star}(M)=\max\{\mu_{1},\mu_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{\star}(M)=\max\{\mu_{1},\mu_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Pick <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and consider <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{1}:(\mu_{1},\mu_{2})\!=\!(+g/2,-g/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}:(\mu_{1},\mu_{2})\!=\!(+g/2,-g/2)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M_{2}\!:\!(\mu_{1},\mu_{2})\!=\!(-g/2,+g/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}\!:\!(\mu_{1},\mu_{2})\!=\!(-g/2,+g/2)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{a}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{a}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of pulls of arm <math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{R}_{T}(M_{1},x)=g\,N_{2}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{R}_{T}(M_{1},x)=g\,N_{2}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{R}_{T}(M_{2},x)=g\,N_{1}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{R}_{T}(M_{2},x)=g\,N_{1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>; thus, <math id="S5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{R}_{T}(M_{1},x)+\mathfrak{R}_{T}(M_{2},x)=g(N_{1}(x)+N_{2}(x))=gT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{R}_{T}(M_{1},x)+\mathfrak{R}_{T}(M_{2},x)=g(N_{1}(x)+N_{2}(x))=gT</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the separation condition holds with <math id="S5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=gT/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=gT/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the trajectory law under <math id="S5.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="S5.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\delta}=\log(1/(4\delta(1-\delta)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\delta}=\log(1/(4\delta(1-\delta)))</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the divergence decomposition lemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Lemma 15.1]</cite>, <math id="S5.p3.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(\mathbb{P}_{1}\|\mathbb{P}_{2})=\sum_{a=1}^{2}\mathbb{E}_{M_{1}}[N_{a}(T)]\,\mathrm{KL}(\mathcal{N}(\mu_{a}^{(1)},1)\|\mathcal{N}(\mu_{a}^{(2)},1))=(g^{2}/2)\,T." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(\mathbb{P}_{1}\|\mathbb{P}_{2})=\sum_{a=1}^{2}\mathbb{E}_{M_{1}}[N_{a}(T)]\,\mathrm{KL}(\mathcal{N}(\mu_{a}^{(1)},1)\|\mathcal{N}(\mu_{a}^{(2)},1))=(g^{2}/2)\,T.</annotation></semantics></math>
Using Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (High-probability Le Cam under interaction (TV and KL)). ‣ 3.3 Tools based on Le Cam’s method. ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, if <math id="S5.p3.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(\mathbb{P}_{1}\|\mathbb{P}_{2})&lt;\Lambda_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(\mathbb{P}_{1}\|\mathbb{P}_{2})&lt;\Lambda_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and the separation holds, then <math id="S5.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, whenever <math id="S5.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{2}T/2&lt;\Lambda_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{2}T/2&lt;\Lambda_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq gT/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq gT/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose any <math id="S5.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and take <math id="S5.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=(1-\eta)\sqrt{2\Lambda_{\delta}/T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=(1-\eta)\sqrt{2\Lambda_{\delta}/T}</annotation></semantics></math>, which yields <math id="S5.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq(1-\eta)\sqrt{(T/2)\Lambda_{\delta}}=(1-\eta)\sqrt{(T/2)\log(1/(4\delta(1-\delta)))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{M}_{-}(\delta)\geq(1-\eta)\sqrt{(T/2)\Lambda_{\delta}}=(1-\eta)\sqrt{(T/2)\log(1/(4\delta(1-\delta)))}</annotation></semantics></math>; letting <math id="S5.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\downarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\downarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> proves the claim.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Chapter 17 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> proves weak-tail bounds <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(L\geq r)\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(L\geq r)\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> with the same two-arm Gaussian scaling <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{T\log(1/\delta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{T\log(1/\delta)}</annotation></semantics></math> (up to constants). In contrast, we obtain a <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-explicit <em id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">strong-tail</em> minimax-quantile lower bound via Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (High-probability Le Cam under interaction (TV and KL)). ‣ 3.3 Tools based on Le Cam’s method. ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>; instantiating it for the two-arm Gaussian bandit yields our bound immediately. Moreover, the same theorem holds in ISDM and provides a template for analogous high-probability lower bounds beyond bandits.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-explicit minimax-quantile lens for ISDM problems and supply interactive Fano and Le Cam tools that are straightforward to instantiate, recovering optimal scaling on two-armed bandits. It would be interesting to extend to the ISDM framework, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Thm. 8]</cite>, which transfers local minimax-risk lower bounds to the minimax quantile. Another future direction is to develop both <em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-explicit</em> weak minimax quantile lower bounds and strict minimax-quantile lower bounds in interactive settings using the quantile-DEC framework, for various problems including episodic RL.</p>
</div>
</section>
